  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "An engineer measures the amount of fuel $F$, in liters, remaining in a generator. The following table shows the rate at which fuel is being consumed.\n\n| **$t\\,\\text{(hours)}$** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${F}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{(liters/hour)}$ | $-3.1$ | $-3.5$ | $-4.0$ | $-4.4$ |\n\nAt $t=4,$ the generator contained $62$ liters of fuel. Use the tangent line approximation of $F$ at time $t=4$ to estimate the amount of fuel remaining at $t=4.6.$  Do not round the answer.\n\nThe amount of fuel remaining at $t=4.6$ hours is estimated to be\n{{field-1}}\nliters.",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $F(t)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =F(a)+{F}^{\u2032}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =F(4)+{F}^{\u2032}(4)(t-4) \\\\  & =62-3.5(t-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $F(4.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $t=4.6.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4.6) & \u2248L(4.6) \\\\  & =62-3.5(4.6-4) \\\\  & =62-3.5(0.6) \\\\  & =62-2.1 \\\\  & =59.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe amount of fuel remaining at $t=4.6$ hours is estimated to be $59.9$ liters.",
